<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>sh.paseo.daemon</string>
	<!--
	  Paseo daemon, started at login so this host is always reachable as a Paseo
	  execution target from the phone / latios / any other client.

	  Installed on ALL Macs in the fleet (kinto, latios, nimbus). Unlike the Orca
	  serve agent this is NOT hostname-gated: Paseo's model is one daemon per
	  execution host, and clients pick which host owns the work. Kinto is the one
	  that matters most (always-on, the host Rai drives from his phone), but a
	  daemon on each is the documented topology.

	  WHY THIS EXISTS: `paseo daemon start` from a shell dies with the session, so
	  without this a reboot silently ends remote control of kinto and Rai finds out
	  only when he reaches for his phone and nothing is there. That is exactly the
	  failure mode the Brita filter rule exists to prevent, so it is automated
	  rather than left as a thing to remember.

	  Direct-exec with an explicit PATH, same reasoning as dev.onorca.serve: a login
	  zsh fires the powerlevel10k instant prompt in launchd's no-tty context and
	  mangles the command. The `paseo` shim is a POSIX sh script that execs the
	  bundled "Paseo Helper" node runtime, so it needs HOME plus a PATH good enough
	  for the agent CLIs (claude/codex/git/node) the daemon launches.

	  --foreground is REQUIRED: the default daemonises and detaches, which would
	  make launchd think the job exited instantly and thrash it against KeepAlive.

	  SECURITY POSTURE (deliberate, do not widen without Rai's say-so):
	    * Listens on 127.0.0.1:6767 only. No inbound port is exposed on any host.
	    * Remote access is via Paseo's outbound relay (E2E encrypted; the relay sees
	      connection metadata, never prompts, code, or responses).
	    * Therefore the PAIRING LINK is the sole trust anchor -- treat it exactly
	      like a password. `paseo daemon pair` prints it; never paste it anywhere.
	    * To go relay-free instead, bind to the tailnet IP and add --no-relay. That
	      is a bigger exposure decision on a work machine and is Rai's call, not a
	      default.
	-->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/Applications/Paseo.app/Contents/Resources/bin/paseo</string>
		<string>daemon</string>
		<string>start</string>
		<string>--foreground</string>
	</array>
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}</string>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/bin:{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/bin:{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/Library/pnpm:{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.asdf/shims:{{ if eq .chezmoi.arch "arm64" }}/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin{{ else }}/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin{{ end }}:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
	</dict>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>10</integer>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/Library/Logs/paseo-daemon.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/Library/Logs/paseo-daemon.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
